panvk: constant arrays a shader indexes are its constant data (nir_opt_large_constants), not thread-local storage

Applied by tools/mk-panvk.sh only, after panvk-host-memory.patch, to Mesa 25.0.7 (Mesa's own licence, MIT, applies to the
lines it adds). Written for Scion.

Why. A constant array that a shader indexes with a value it computes (a noise function's permutation table, say) stays a
function-temporary variable through PanVK's lowering, and pan_shader_preprocess() (bifrost_preprocess_nir) puts every such
array of more than 256 bytes in thread-local storage: each invocation first stores the whole array there, then reads the
few words it needs. The Astronomy wallpaper's fragment shaders (NanoUniverse's aegir_*_fsh, which AegirPoster draws with)
each build a 4 KiB table so, 256 vec4 stores a pixel: 2304 store_scratch in one frame's shaders, 294 load/store cycles
a pixel by BIFROST_MESA_DEBUG=shaderdb. A frame of the Earth took about 20 s at 1170x2532, and its fragment job was
killed by HakiosMali at its 1 s limit (status 0x8), which reset the GPU for every client (scion-kernel docs/metal.md,
phases 7 to 9).

What. nir_opt_large_constants moves arrays that are only ever written with constants into the shader's constant data
(nir_shader::constant_data), after nir_lower_global_vars_to_local and before pan_shader_preprocess(), and each
load_constant becomes a load_global_constant at that data's address, which the shader reads as a sysval
(panvk_{graphics,compute}_sysvals::const_data). The data is uploaded beside the binary (panvk_shader::const_data_mem) and
serialized with it; the sysval is set to the bound shader's address as its push uniforms are made, so the vertex and
fragment shaders of a draw each get their own. With it, no store_scratch is left in those shaders, the load/store cycles
are 10 to 108 a pixel, and the Earth's frame takes 0.7 s at 1170x2532 (the rest of the scene 70 ms).

